kT_{\\rm C}\\sim\\frac{U_{\\gamma}}{n_{\\gamma}}=m_{p}c^{2}\\frac{n_{p}}{n_{\\gamma} }\\frac{U_{\\gamma}}{\\rho c^{2}}\\sim 10\\,{\\rm keV},  where n_{\\gamma}/n_{p}=10^{4} is the photon number per proton in the homologous envelope and k is the Boltzmann constant. We find that photon production by double Compton scattering and bremsstrahlung from the heated plasma behind the shock do not significantly change the photon number, so that n_{\\gamma}/n_{p} stays constant. The magnetic field in the homologous envelope is expected to be very weak (BLL20). Therefore, the RMS does not have any significant collisionless subshock that would form in a magnetized plasma (Beloborodov, 2017) and produce synchrotron photons (Lundman & Beloborodov, 2019).

